// SOS kernel entry.
//
// The per-arch kernel HAL (`hal/<arch>/kernel/`) supplies the boot shim (stack,
// .bss zeroing, the call to `kmain`), the board hooks, the memory-protection
// primitive and the linker script that places the image. `sosrt`
// (`rt/common/src/lib.saw`) supplies the runtime seams and the arena over those
// hooks. `core/` is the arch-free kernel (console, `ktrap`, the object-op
// dispatch, the sosimg loader), one module per seam with `core/lib.saw` as the
// facade. See spec.md §5b and §12.
//
// This file names no architecture: the same entry boots every profile, and the
// build only chooses which HAL `hal` maps to.
//
// At boot the kernel hands control to the root server: load the one appended
// image, grant it its regions, drop to user mode. With no root image there is
// nothing to run, and the kernel says so rather than exiting quietly.

import kcore.{console, load_root_and_enter}
import hal

@export("kmain")
func kmain() {
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS M1: kernel up on ")
    uart.write_str(hal.arch_name())
    uart.write_str("\n")

    // The interrupt controller comes up with every line masked. Binding an
    // Interrupt (`SystemOp.InterruptBind`) unmasks one line and nothing else,
    // so the controller must already be initialised; masked, nothing is
    // deliverable until a driver asks.
    //
    // No timer is armed here. The scheduler works without one (a `yield` and a
    // block both switch), and a tick would narrate over the process for the
    // first few ticks; see the handover protocol on `kcore`'s `Console`.
    hal.intc_init()

    load_root_and_enter()
}
